 \subsection{Relation to existing work}
-Continuous primal--dual range inference~\cite{stark}, computational optimal recovery~\cite{ettehad}, deterministic-to-statistical moduli~\cite{donoho}, and honest intervals on local performance classes~\cite{cailow} provide the foundations for recovering a functional from incomplete data. Brown and Grabovsky~\cite{browngrabovsky} establish sharp extrapolation exponents and local extremality conditions for completely monotone functions under relative $L^2$ discrepancy. Their extrapolated value is a linear functional of a representing positive measure. Here the original componentwise RC constraints lead to a sharp comparison of finite designs, the full frequency axis, and sequential experiments.
-
-Positive-measure uniqueness in Chebyshev systems is established by Eftekhari et al.~\cite[Proposition~8]{eftekhari}. Proposition~\ref{prop:frequencycount} resolves the exact frequency count when two conic calibration coordinates accompany the RC measurements. Wu and Yang~\cite[Proposition~5]{wuyang} and Nguyen et al.~\cite[Theorem~5 and Appendix~A.6]{nguyen} develop cluster-sensitive moment and mixture geometry. Building on these interpolation principles, we obtain spatially varying target-regularity moduli and match them to positive alternatives that are uniformly close across the entire measurement family. The Fourier comparison in Remark~\ref{rem:fourier} identifies the role of the RC kernel's uniform smoothing, alongside positive Fourier super-resolution results~\cite{liuammari}.
-
-Adaptive-information comparisons~\cite{knu} and noisy-information bounds through entropy numbers~\cite{knpu} establish the importance of the information class and its normalization. Our information consists of RC evaluations, with a prescribed error floor or precision cost. The full-frequency response pair supplies the lower bound for this restricted family. Stieltjes reconstruction~\cite{grabovsky} and circuit-parameter frequency design~\cite{sovljanski} provide complementary reconstruction and design perspectives; Section~\ref{sec:experiments} quantifies the constant-factor gains available within the matching asymptotic order.
-
-We combine stopped change of measure~\cite{castro,kaufmann,garivier} and time-uniform coverage~\cite{howard} with polynomial semi-infinite optimization~\cite{papp}, continuous exchange refinement~\cite{grid}, and verified finite optimization~\cite{jansson,schmid}. The resulting contribution is a sharp, attained information and acquisition law for calibrated relaxation functionals, together with a finite numerical realization of the corresponding continuous ranges.
