 \section{Conclusions}
-We determine the minimal frequency design, sharp information order, and attainable acquisition cost for calibrated relaxation functionals. Exactly $K+2$ distinct frequencies provide uniform noiseless identification of references with at most $K$ atoms among arbitrary positive competitors when resistance and inductance are unknown; the threshold is $K+1$ with known calibration. At the calibrated threshold, a fixed finite design attains the same worst-class functional accuracy order as the entire positive-frequency response. Local target regularity and cluster multiplicity determine this order uniformly through vanishing masses and colliding atoms.
-
-The same modulus yields matching fixed-width, fixed-confidence acquisition costs against adaptive choices of frequency, precision, and stopping. A class-tuned expanding design attains the optimal order and completes almost surely at every fixed finite positive spectrum. Positive projection, verified primal--dual gaps, and finite-budget continuous checks turn the information bounds into certified functional ranges. The a posteriori width criterion controls numerical enlargement directly from feasible witnesses and dual certificates. Together, these results establish a complete path from the minimal identifying design to a rigorously computed recovery interval at the requested accuracy.
+The analysis determines the frequency information and measurement effort required to compute calibrated relaxation functionals. Exactly $K+2$ distinct frequencies give uniform noiseless identification of references with at most $K$ atoms among arbitrary positive competitors when resistance and inductance are unknown; known calibration reduces the count to $K+1$. At the calibrated threshold, a fixed design attains the entire positive-frequency response's worst-class functional accuracy order. Local target regularity and cluster multiplicity determine this order uniformly through colliding atoms and vanishing masses.
+
+The continuous range calculation makes the information law computationally accessible. Positive projection gives a strict-feasibility guard, verified primal--dual pairs separate finite optimization from continuum correction, and a finite refinement schedule returns both endpoints at any prescribed numerical excess. Certified width brackets additionally provide absolute--relative accuracy control from the data themselves. The same range calculation drives a sequential design with matching expected acquisition cost under adaptive frequency, precision, and stopping alternatives. Together, the results provide a constructive account of minimal identification, sharp recovery, and verified computation for positive relaxation measures.
 
